// PARAM: --analysis interval
// Rejection case. Compilation resolves every name through one flat kind
// environment, so a name carrying two kinds has no representation there:
// whichever entry came first would silently win and the other procedure
// would be analysed at a kind its source never wrote. Rejecting names the
// conflict instead of answering wrongly.
//
// The restriction is the flat environment's, not the language's. Scoped
// identities would let these two coexist; until compilation resolves through
// them, this is the honest boundary.
void f(int32 n) { uint8 acc; acc := n; return }
void g(int32 n) { int64 acc; acc := n; return }
void main() { int32 a; a := 1 }
